  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-074899aeeeffabc6": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-102",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Though so much care was taken in ancient Egypt to embalm the body, there were many who were buried without coffins. The mention of the fact here that “Joseph was put in a coffin,” shows the high rank to which he had attained. His coffin was probably the outside receptacle or sarc…"
      },
      "c-b60e7b380b60d23f": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-101",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The “threshing-floor” was not a shed, or a building, or any place covered with roof and surrounded by walls, but a circular piece of ground from fifty to a hundred feet in diameter, in the open air, on elevated ground, and made smooth, hardy, and clean. Here the grain was threshe…"
      },
      "c-c4844bb3a8a9ce5c": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-100",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "This not only shows the high esteem in which Joseph was held, but it also furnishes an illustration of the Egyptian fashion of large and stately funeral processions. The custom existed in every province in Egypt, and in every age of its history."
      },
      "c-c5eee38f00e33122": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-99",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The reason why Joseph did not himself prefer his request to the king, but solicited the intervention of his friends, is to be found in the fact that, having allowed his hair and beard to grow during the seventy days of mourning, he was not in a condition to appear before Pharaoh…"
      },
      "c-ec15c4502df76134": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-98",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Through this incision the viscera were drawn with the exception of the heart and kidneys. They were sometimes replaced after being prepared for preservation, and in other instances were put into vases. Some authorities assert that they were thrown into the river Nile; but this is…"
      }
    },
